{"id": "6f463e43-88d7-4f3c-b783-44a45640980f", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This EKS compliance check is bounded but broad, scanning cluster configuration without an obvious root-cause investigation needed, so it's a good candidate to delegate to a sub-agent for token efficiency. There's no existing skill for EKS compliance, so I'll outline my plan to the user and hand this off, using the connected AWS account (012345678901_1) as the target.

I'll craft a thorough prompt for the sub-agent covering CIS Amazon EKS Benchmark checks with an honest crosswalk between what's verifiable and what isn't given read-only access, preloading relevant skills like verifying-with-docs. Given the depth the user wants, I should flag this as deep research mode with the proper marker line before delegating.

"}]}

{"id": "1b283327-75ad-4920-88d9-0e59c7d3d5e2", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll run the CIS-style EKS checks against cis-skill-test in account 012345678901_1, us-east-1. That covers the control plane, node groups, networking, IAM/IRSA, and in-cluster RBAC and pod security. Then I'll map the results to NIST SP 800-53, PCI DSS, SOC 2, and HIPAA. The scan reads a lot of configuration, so I'm running it in the background and will bring back the full crosswalk when it's done.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_qx40TNnUBBS1xXltc6lFCe", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "eks-compliance-crosswalk", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["discovering-topology", "searching-logs", "verifying-with-docs", "investigating-infrastructure-changes"], "prompt": "[DEEP RESEARCH MODE]
{
  "task": "Run a read-only compliance check of EKS cluster 'cis-skill-test' (us-east-1, AWS account 012345678901_1, the connected account) against the CIS Amazon EKS Benchmark, then produce a COMPLETE crosswalk to NIST SP 800-53 Rev 5, PCI DSS v4.0, SOC 2 (Trust Services Criteria, e.g. CC6.x/CC7.x/CC8.x), and HIPAA Security Rule (45 CFR 164.3xx).",
  "context": "User wants EVERY crosswalk row for ALL FOUR frameworks, each with: row/check ID and name, overall status (PASS / FAIL / NOT VERIFIED / NOT APPLICABLE), the mapped control IDs for each framework AND the status of each mapped control, and the basis (the concrete evidence: API call/kubectl command, the observed value, or the reason it could not be checked). Do not omit rows and do not summarize them away. Do not invent evidence: if something cannot be checked (e.g. kubectl access denied, node-level kubelet files not reachable, no SSM access), mark it NOT VERIFIED and state exactly why. You are read-only; do not attempt any mutating call.",
  "resources": ["EKS cluster cis-skill-test", "account 012345678901_1", "region us-east-1"],
  "criteria": "Cover at minimum, using eks describe_cluster, list/describe_nodegroups, fargate profiles, addons, access entries/aws-auth, EC2 (security groups, launch templates, IMDS settings, EBS encryption, AMI), IAM (OIDC provider, IRSA roles, node role policies, read-only), KMS, ECR (scan on push, tag immutability, repo policy), CloudWatch log groups, VPC/subnets/endpoints, and kubectl against the cluster (RBAC cluster-admin bindings, wildcard roles, default service account usage/automount, secrets usage, Pod Security Admission labels/policies, network policies per namespace, privileged/hostNetwork/hostPID pods, runAsNonRoot, resource limits, image sources/latest tags, namespaces in use). CIS EKS areas: 2 Logging (all 5 control-plane log types enabled), 3 Worker node config (kubelet settings where verifiable, file perms if verifiable), 4 Policies (RBAC and service accounts, Pod Security Standards, CNI/network policy, secrets management incl. secrets encryption with KMS envelope encryption, general policies/default namespace), 5 Managed services (ECR image scanning, ECR access, cluster access: private endpoint, public endpoint CIDR restriction, private nodes, network policy support, KMS secrets encryption, dedicated service accounts/IRSA, IMDSv2 / hop limit, Bottlerocket/container-optimized AMI). Also check supported Kubernetes version and endpoint public access CIDRs. For framework mappings, use well-known control IDs (e.g., NIST AC-2, AC-3, AC-6, AU-2, AU-12, CM-6, CM-7, SC-7, SC-8, SC-12, SC-13, SC-28, SI-2, SI-4, IA-2, IA-5, RA-5; PCI DSS 1.x, 2.2, 3.5/3.6, 4.2, 6.3, 7.x, 8.x, 10.x, 11.x; SOC 2 CC6.1, CC6.6, CC6.7, CC7.1, CC7.2, CC8.1; HIPAA 164.312(a)(1), (a)(2)(iv), (b), (c)(1), (d), (e)(1), 164.308(a)(1)(ii)(A/B), (a)(3), (a)(4), (a)(5), 164.310). Each mapped control's status should be derived from the CIS rows mapped to it (FAIL if any mapped row FAILs, PASS only if all mapped rows PASS, else PARTIAL/NOT VERIFIED), and state that mapping logic. Return output as: (1) short cluster facts summary; (2) the full crosswalk table with one row per check and columns: ID, check, status, NIST 800-53 controls (with status), PCI DSS controls (with status), SOC 2 criteria (with status), HIPAA citations (with status), basis; (3) a per-framework rollup listing each control ID with its derived status and the contributing rows; (4) list of items NOT VERIFIED and why; (5) caveat that this is a technical control mapping, not a formal audit attestation. Return the entire table in your final report, not a truncated sample."
}
"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Scan EKS cluster cis-skill-test and build compliance crosswalk"}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "f87b926c-baa4-4206-9bd1-1519f0a1ce05", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I notice there's a Kubernetes access entry tied to a "devops-agent-cis-readers" group, but since I don't have a kubectl tool available, I can't actually verify kubectl access—so that stays unverified despite the access entry existing, since the RBAC binding for that group is unknown. I'm wondering if direct API calls might be a way around this.

Actually, use_aws is boto3-only, so there's no path to the K8s API or eks.get_token that way—still unverified. I should check other areas: the control plane security group, IRSA and OIDC provider status, KMS encryption config on the cluster, ECR repo policies and scan findings, node IAM/SSM access, node public IPs, GuardDuty runtime monitoring, other access entries' policies, and the cluster's Kubernetes version against supported versions given the current date.

"}], "parent_id": "03683f76-59f0-4914-b072-d8034b530012"}

